1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,402
You're holding a stick of RAM from an old PC, and there's a brand new motherboard on your desk.

2
00:00:06,682 --> 00:00:09,935
Will it fit, and if it somehow did, would it even work?

3
00:00:10,215 --> 00:00:20,135
To answer that, we'll follow this one stick through three generations of memory, and then into every other place RAM hides inside your devices.

4
00:00:20,415 --> 00:00:30,134
By the end, you'll be able to look at any memory stick, laptop or graphics card and know what kind of RAM it uses, and why it can't be swapped.

5
00:00:30,884 --> 00:00:32,997
Start with what's actually on the stick.

6
00:00:33,277 --> 00:00:36,286
Those black rectangles are DRAM chips.

7
00:00:36,566 --> 00:00:48,006
Each one stores billions of bits as tiny electrical charges, and because those charges slowly leak away, the chips are refreshed over and over just to remember anything.

8
00:00:48,286 --> 00:00:51,303
The letters DDR stand for double data rate.

9
00:00:51,583 --> 00:01:01,503
The memory runs on a clock, a steady tick, and DDR moves data twice on every tick, once as the signal rises and once as it falls.

10
00:01:01,783 --> 00:01:06,604
That's why speeds are measured in megatransfers per second, not megahertz.

11
00:01:06,884 --> 00:01:14,290
A stick sold as DDR4-3200 has a clock of 1600 megahertz.

12
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:16,859
It just moves data on both edges.

13
00:01:17,609 --> 00:01:19,488
Now look at the label on our stick.

14
00:01:19,768 --> 00:01:21,383
It says DDR3.

15
00:01:21,663 --> 00:01:32,877
That standard launched in 2007, and it ran at 1.5 volts, with a low voltage version, DDR3L, at 1.35 volts.

16
00:01:33,157 --> 00:01:39,345
Official speeds went from 800 up to 2133 megatransfers per second.

17
00:01:39,625 --> 00:01:47,326
Run your finger along the bottom edge and you'll count 240 gold contacts, with one small gap cut into them.

18
00:01:47,606 --> 00:01:48,826
Remember that gap.

19
00:01:49,106 --> 00:01:50,289
It's about to matter.

20
00:01:51,039 --> 00:01:56,373
The next generation, DDR4, reached PCs in 2014.

21
00:01:56,653 --> 00:02:08,182
It dropped the voltage to 1.2 volts and started at 2133 megatransfers per second, with the official standard going up to 3200.

22
00:02:08,462 --> 00:02:23,558
The stick is the same length as DDR3, but it has 288 contacts packed tighter, and its bottom edge is slightly curved, so the pins touch the slot a few at a time and the stick takes less force to push in.

23
00:02:23,838 --> 00:02:26,867
And that little gap, the notch, sits in a different spot.

24
00:02:27,617 --> 00:02:29,814
That's the answer to our first question.

25
00:02:30,094 --> 00:02:31,369
The notch is a key.

26
00:02:31,649 --> 00:02:40,914
Each DDR generation puts it in a different place, and the slot has a matching ridge, so an older stick physically can't seat in a newer slot.

27
00:02:41,194 --> 00:02:42,349
That's on purpose.

28
00:02:42,629 --> 00:02:53,645
The voltages are different, the signals are different, and the memory controller that talks to the RAM lives inside the processor, built for a specific generation.

29
00:02:53,925 --> 00:03:04,059
Some Intel chips, like the twelfth generation, can handle both DDR4 and DDR5, but any single motherboard is wired for one.

30
00:03:04,339 --> 00:03:06,490
So our DDR3 stick is out.

31
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:08,241
Let's see what replaced it.

32
00:03:08,991 --> 00:03:13,604
DDR5 was published as a standard in 2020.

33
00:03:13,884 --> 00:03:25,607
It starts where DDR4 topped out and then some, at 4800 megatransfers per second, and the official speed list now runs up to 8800.

34
00:03:25,887 --> 00:03:29,012
The voltage drops again, to 1.1 volts.

35
00:03:29,292 --> 00:03:30,335
Here's the trap.

36
00:03:30,615 --> 00:03:36,965
A DDR5 stick has the same 288 contacts as DDR4.

37
00:03:37,245 --> 00:03:38,715
Same count, same length.

38
00:03:38,995 --> 00:03:45,544
Only the notch has moved, and that's the only thing stopping you from forcing the wrong stick into the wrong board.

39
00:03:46,294 --> 00:03:49,681
Inside, DDR5 changed how the stick talks.

40
00:03:49,961 --> 00:03:57,365
A DDR4 stick is one 64-bit channel, like one wide road where everything travels together.

41
00:03:57,645 --> 00:04:08,351
A DDR5 stick splits that into two independent 32-bit subchannels, two narrower roads that can each carry a different request at the same time.

42
00:04:08,631 --> 00:04:18,912
Each subchannel sends longer bursts, sixteen transfers instead of eight, so one request still delivers the same 64 byte chunk the processor wants.

43
00:04:19,192 --> 00:04:28,204
The result is that one stick can juggle more small jobs at once, which is exactly what modern processors with lots of cores ask for.

44
00:04:28,954 --> 00:04:33,312
DDR5 also moved some parts onto the stick itself.

45
00:04:33,592 --> 00:04:38,180
On older generations, the motherboard regulated the memory's power.

46
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:49,939
A DDR5 stick carries its own power management chip, which takes in 5 volts on desktop sticks and turns it into the exact voltages the memory chips need.

47
00:04:50,219 --> 00:04:58,235
Each chip also has on-die ECC, error correction built inside the chip that fixes flipped bits before they leave it.

48
00:04:58,515 --> 00:05:05,403
That's not the same as the full ECC memory servers use, which also protects the trip to the processor.

49
00:05:05,683 --> 00:05:12,658
And because the chips got denser, the largest possible stick grew from 64 gigabytes to 512.

50
00:05:13,408 --> 00:05:15,777
Now take the same question to a laptop.

51
00:05:16,057 --> 00:05:25,996
Laptop sticks are called SO-DIMMs, and they're about half the length of a desktop stick, roughly 68 millimeters instead of 133.

52
00:05:26,276 --> 00:05:27,726
The same rules apply.

53
00:05:28,006 --> 00:05:39,532
A DDR4 SO-DIMM has 260 contacts, a DDR5 one has 262, the notches differ, and neither fits the other's slot.

54
00:05:39,812 --> 00:05:46,912
So if your laptop has SO-DIMM slots, you can upgrade it, but only with the generation it was built for.

55
00:05:47,662 --> 00:05:50,244
Many thin laptops don't have slots at all.

56
00:05:50,524 --> 00:05:57,859
They use LPDDR, low power DDR, which is soldered to the board right next to the processor.

57
00:05:58,139 --> 00:06:05,886
Shorter wires mean less power and higher speeds, but you can never upgrade it, so buy the capacity you'll need on day one.

58
00:06:06,166 --> 00:06:13,322
A newer removable module called LPCAMM2 tries to give you both, and a few laptops already use it.

59
00:06:13,602 --> 00:06:15,400
Then there's your graphics card.

60
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:24,237
The chips around its GPU are GDDR, graphics DDR, built for raw bandwidth instead of low latency.

61
00:06:24,517 --> 00:06:30,100
GDDR6 reaches up to 24 gigabits per second on every pin.

62
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:39,957
GDDR7 starts at 32 and is rated up to 48, partly by sending three voltage levels per signal instead of two.

63
00:06:40,707 --> 00:06:42,589
So, back to the stick in your hand.

64
00:06:42,869 --> 00:06:47,617
It's DDR3, so it won't fit the new board, and it isn't supposed to.

65
00:06:47,897 --> 00:06:54,019
What does fit is whatever your motherboard's spec page lists, and it lists exactly one generation.

66
00:06:54,299 --> 00:07:05,941
Buy a matched kit of that type, at least two sticks so you get two channels, and remember that a new kit starts at a safe default speed until you turn on its speed profile in the BIOS.

67
00:07:06,221 --> 00:07:07,498
That's the whole family.

68
00:07:07,778 --> 00:07:21,631
DDR for desktops, SO-DIMMs for laptops, LPDDR soldered for battery life, and GDDR on your graphics card, each one keyed, timed and powered for its own job.

69
00:07:22,381 --> 00:07:23,529
But here's the thing.

70
00:07:23,809 --> 00:07:29,643
Even the fastest RAM won't help if memory isn't what's holding your PC back.

71
00:07:29,923 --> 00:07:36,504
Next time, we'll figure out why your PC is slow, and why more RAM usually isn't the fix.

72
00:07:36,784 --> 00:07:38,465
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

